  title="How to Solve Quadratic Equations — 3 Methods with Examples | MathsIsCool"
  description="Learn all 3 methods to solve quadratic equations: factoring, quadratic formula, and completing the square. Step-by-step worked examples for Class 10, GCSE and A-Level."
  canonical="/blog/how-to-solve-quadratic-equations"
>
<div class="page">
  <div class="page-hero">
    <div style="font-size:3rem;margin-bottom:8px;">📐</div>
    <h1 style="color:var(--color-algebra)">How to Solve Quadratic Equations</h1>
    <p>Three methods explained with full worked examples — factoring, quadratic formula, and completing the square.</p>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:8px;justify-content:center;margin-top:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
      <span style="background:#D9770615;color:#D97706;border:1px solid #D9770630;font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:700;font-family:'Nunito',sans-serif;padding:3px 12px;border-radius:100px;">⏱️ 8 min read</span>
      <span style="background:#D9770615;color:#D97706;border:1px solid #D9770630;font-size:0.78rem;font-weight:700;font-family:'Nunito',sans-serif;padding:3px 12px;border-radius:100px;">Algebra</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="owl-tip">
    <picture><source srcset="/images/owl-main.webp" type="image/webp" /><img src="/images/owl-main-opt.png" alt="Professor Hoot" class="owl-tip-img" width="56" height="56" loading="lazy" /></picture>
    <div class="owl-tip-content">
      <strong>🦉 Professor Hoot Says:</strong>
      <p>Every quadratic equation can be solved — the question is just which method is fastest for that particular equation. Learn all three and choose wisely!</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="seo-content" style="border-top:none;padding-top:0;">
    <p>A quadratic equation is any equation of the form <strong>ax² + bx + c = 0</strong> where a ≠ 0. Quadratic equations appear throughout mathematics — from projectile motion in physics to profit maximization in economics. There are three main methods to solve them, each suited to different types of equations.</p>

    <h2>Method 1 — Factoring (Fastest When It Works)</h2>
    <p>Factoring works when the quadratic can be neatly split into two brackets. It is the fastest method when applicable.</p>
    <p><strong>Example: Solve x² + 5x + 6 = 0</strong></p>
    <p>Find two numbers that multiply to give 6 (the constant) and add to give 5 (the coefficient of x). Those numbers are 2 and 3, since 2×3=6 and 2+3=5.</p>
    <p>So: x² + 5x + 6 = (x + 2)(x + 3) = 0</p>
    <p>Therefore: x + 2 = 0 → <strong>x = -2</strong>, or x + 3 = 0 → <strong>x = -3</strong></p>
    <p>When factoring does not work cleanly (no obvious integer pairs), use Method 2 or 3.</p>

    <h2>Method 2 — The Quadratic Formula (Always Works)</h2>
    <p>The quadratic formula works for <em>any</em> quadratic equation, even when factoring is impossible:</p>
    <div style="background:var(--surface);border:2px solid var(--color-algebra);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:20px;text-align:center;margin:16px 0;">
      <div style="font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--color-algebra);">x = (−b ± √(b²−4ac)) / 2a</div>
    </div>
    <p><strong>Example: Solve 2x² + 3x − 2 = 0</strong></p>
    <p>Here a=2, b=3, c=−2. Substituting: x = (−3 ± √(9+16)) / 4 = (−3 ± √25) / 4 = (−3 ± 5) / 4</p>
    <p>x = (−3+5)/4 = 2/4 = <strong>1/2</strong>, or x = (−3−5)/4 = −8/4 = <strong>−2</strong></p>

    <h2>Method 3 — Completing the Square</h2>
    <p>Completing the square is used when you need the equation in vertex form, for derivations, or when the quadratic formula is not permitted. It is also how the quadratic formula itself is derived.</p>
    <p><strong>Example: Solve x² + 6x + 5 = 0 by completing the square</strong></p>
    <p>Step 1: Move constant to right: x² + 6x = −5</p>
    <p>Step 2: Add (6/2)² = 9 to both sides: x² + 6x + 9 = 4</p>
    <p>Step 3: Write left side as a square: (x+3)² = 4</p>
    <p>Step 4: Square root both sides: x+3 = ±2</p>
    <p>Therefore: x = −3+2 = <strong>−1</strong>, or x = −3−2 = <strong>−5</strong></p>

    <h2>The Discriminant — How Many Solutions?</h2>
    <p>The expression b²−4ac inside the quadratic formula is called the <strong>discriminant</strong> (symbol: Δ). It tells you how many real solutions exist before you solve:</p>
    <p>Δ > 0 → Two distinct real solutions | Δ = 0 → One repeated solution | Δ &lt; 0 → No real solutions (complex roots)</p>

    <h2>Which Method Should You Use?</h2>
    <p>Use <strong>factoring</strong> first — if you can spot the factors quickly, it is fastest. Use the <strong>quadratic formula</strong> when factoring is not obvious or when coefficients are large. Use <strong>completing the square</strong> when explicitly required by the question or when dealing with inequalities and vertex form.</p>

    <h2>More Worked Examples</h2>
    <h3>Factoring when a is not 1</h3>
    <p><strong>Solve 2x² + 7x + 3 = 0.</strong> Multiply a × c = 2 × 3 = 6, then find two numbers that multiply to 6 and add to 7: they are 6 and 1. Split the middle term: 2x² + 6x + x + 3 = 2x(x + 3) + 1(x + 3) = (2x + 1)(x + 3). So <strong>x = −1/2</strong> or <strong>x = −3</strong>.</p>
    <h3>Quadratic formula with an irrational answer</h3>
    <p><strong>Solve x² − 4x + 1 = 0.</strong> Here a = 1, b = −4, c = 1. Then x = (4 ± √(16 − 4)) / 2 = (4 ± √12) / 2 = (4 ± 2√3) / 2 = <strong>2 ± √3</strong>. This cannot be factored with whole numbers, which is exactly when the formula is useful.</p>
    <h3>Using the discriminant</h3>
    <p><strong>Does x² + 2x + 5 = 0 have real solutions?</strong> The discriminant is b² − 4ac = 4 − 20 = −16, which is negative. So there are <strong>no real solutions</strong>.</p>

    <h2>Sum and Product of Roots (Shortcut)</h2>
    <p>For ax² + bx + c = 0 with roots p and q, the sum of the roots is p + q = −b/a and the product is p × q = c/a. Check with x² + 5x + 6 = 0: the roots are −2 and −3, their sum is −5 (which is −b/a) and their product is 6 (which is c/a). This is handy for checking your answers quickly.</p>

    <h2>Real-Life Example</h2>
    <p>A ball is thrown upward and its height in metres after t seconds is h = −5t² + 20t. When does it hit the ground again? Set h = 0: −5t² + 20t = 0, so −5t(t − 4) = 0. Therefore t = 0 (when it is thrown) or <strong>t = 4 seconds</strong> (when it lands).</p>

    <h2>Common Mistakes</h2>
    <p><strong>Not setting the equation to zero first.</strong> Always rewrite so one side is 0 before factoring or using the formula. <strong>Sign errors with b.</strong> If b is negative, then −b becomes positive in the formula. <strong>Dividing by x.</strong> Never divide both sides by x, because you lose the solution x = 0. <strong>Forgetting the ± sign.</strong> A quadratic usually has two answers, not one.</p>

    <h2>Practice Questions</h2>
    <p>Try each one, then open the answer.</p>
    <details><summary>1. x² − 7x + 12 = 0</summary><p>Answer: (x − 3)(x − 4) = 0, so x = 3 or x = 4</p></details>
    <details><summary>2. x² + 4x − 5 = 0</summary><p>Answer: (x + 5)(x − 1) = 0, so x = −5 or x = 1</p></details>
    <details><summary>3. 3x² − 5x − 2 = 0</summary><p>Answer: (3x + 1)(x − 2) = 0, so x = −1/3 or x = 2</p></details>
    <details><summary>4. x² − 6x + 9 = 0</summary><p>Answer: (x − 3)² = 0, so x = 3 (one repeated root, discriminant = 0)</p></details>
    <p>Many quadratics factor neatly using the <a href="/algebra-identities">algebra identities</a>, for example x² − 6x + 9 is (a − b)² and x² − 16 is a² − b². For memory help, see <a href="/blog/algebra-identities-tricks">algebra identity tricks</a>.</p>

    <div class="faq-section" style="margin-top:32px;">
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:16px;">Quadratic Equations — FAQ</h2>
      {[
        ["What is a quadratic equation?","A quadratic equation is an equation of the form ax²+bx+c=0 where a≠0. The highest power of x is 2, which is what makes it quadratic (from Latin quadratus, meaning square)."],
        ["What is the quadratic formula?","x = (−b ± √(b²−4ac)) / 2a. This formula gives both solutions to any quadratic equation directly."],
        ["Can a quadratic equation have no solution?","Yes — when the discriminant b²−4ac is negative, there are no real solutions. The equation has two complex (imaginary) solutions instead."],
        ["What does discriminant mean?","The discriminant is b²−4ac. It tells you the nature of the roots: positive = 2 real roots, zero = 1 repeated root, negative = no real roots."],
      ].map(([q,a]) => (
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q"><span>{q}</span><span class="faq-icon">▾</span></div>
          <div class="faq-a">{a}</div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

